      "sections": [
        {
          "h": "The real cost difference",
          "html": "<p>DigitalOcean's $4 Droplet has no backups, no managed security, no staging and no one to call when PHP breaks. Recreating the Cloudways feature set on DigitalOcean means paying for snapshots, configuring firewalls, installing server management tools — and spending your own hours. For a business site, the $7 difference usually pays for itself in the first incident.</p>"
        },
        {
          "h": "When DigitalOcean still makes sense",
          "html": "<p>Developers who enjoy managing servers, or teams with dedicated DevOps, can save money on DigitalOcean's raw prices. Cloudways runs on DigitalOcean infrastructure too — so you keep DO datacenters and get the managed layer on top.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "verdict": "<p><b>Winner: Cloudways.</b> For anyone running a business website, agency or store, Cloudways delivers the same DigitalOcean hardware plus everything DigitalOcean leaves out — backups, security, staging, scaling and 24/7 support — for a small premium. It is the safer default pick.</p>",
